NAME
       fsck - check and repair a file system

SYNOPSIS
       fsck [-A] [-N] [-R] [-V] [-f] [-n] [-p] [-y] [DEVICE...]

DESCRIPTION
       fsck finds out which type of file system is on DEVICE and runs the
       matching checker: fsck.ext4 (e2fsck) for ext2/3/4, fsck.vfat for FAT.
       The checkers fix what they can and report what they found.

       Only check file systems that are not mounted.

       xfs is different: fsck.xfs does nothing, because xfs checks itself
       when it is mounted. To repair a damaged xfs file system, unmount it
       and run xfs_repair.

       The last field of an /etc/fstab line (the pass number) says whether
       the system runs fsck on it at boot: 1 for the root file system, 2 for
       others, 0 for never. For xfs, use 0.

OPTIONS
       -A     Check every file system listed in /etc/fstab.

       -N     Only show what would be done.

       -R     With -A, skip the root file system.

       -V     Show the commands that are run.

       -f, -n, -p, -y
              Passed on to the checker: force a check, answer no, repair
              automatically, answer yes (see e2fsck).

EXAMPLES
           umount /data
           fsck -f /dev/vdb1

SEE ALSO
       e2fsck(8), fsck.vfat(8), fsck.xfs(8), xfs_repair(8), fstab(5)
